# Rusting: iron needs air AND water, and salt makes it faster
#
# Four nails, four conditions, one clock. Everything here is safe, but the
# vessels are sealed on purpose: the oxygen a nail can reach is the oxygen
# trapped over it, and running out of it is half the lesson.
register lv1

# 1. Dry. Iron and air, no water at all. Predict before you wait.
add v1 Fe 1g
seal v1 500mL
look v1

# 2. Water with the air swept out. A nitrogen purge is the bench's version
#    of the boiled water the classroom experiment uses.
new beaker
add v2 water 100mL
add v2 Fe 1g
sweep v2 1bar
seal v2 500mL

# 3. Plain water, sealed over ordinary room air.
new beaker
add v3 water 100mL
add v3 Fe 1g
seal v3 500mL

# 4. The same, in salt water. Chloride is not used up by the reaction and
#    does not appear in its equation — it makes the same reaction faster.
new beaker
add v4 water 100mL
add v4 NaCl 3g
add v4 Fe 1g
seal v4 500mL

# One day passes for all four.
wait 24h
look v1
look v2
look v3
look v4

# Vessels 1 and 2 are the controls, and each is missing one thing. Read the
# oxygen line in vessels 3 and 4: the salt one has used almost all of it, and
# when the oxygen is gone the rusting stops with iron still in the vessel.
# That is why a sealed tin can does not rust from the inside.
inspect v1
inspect v2
inspect v3
inspect v4

# What this does not model: iron amount stands in for iron area, so filings
# and a nail of the same mass rust at the same speed here; the product is the
# registry's anhydrous iron(III) oxide rather than the hydrated rust of a real
# nail; and an open vessel does not draw oxygen from the room, which is why
# every vessel above is sealed over a measured amount of it.
